<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Chapter 5: Logic (Part 1)</title>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Vocabulary and Notation</title>

        <tabular>
            <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
            <col halign="left" width="20%"/>
            <col halign="left" width="60%"/>
            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Word</cell>
                <cell>Symbol</cell>
                <cell>Meaning</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Statement</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    <line>
                        A <em>statement</em> is a sentence or mathematical expression 
                        </line>
                    <line> that is true or false. </line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Open Sentence</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    <line>    An <em>open sentence</em> is a sentence or mathematical
                        expression </line>
                    <line> which does not have a truth value, depends on
                        some unknown, </line>
                    <line> becomes a statement  when the unknown is
                        specified. </line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>And</cell>
                <cell><m>\wedge</m></cell>
                <cell>
                    <m>P\wedge Q</m> is true when both <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are
                    true.
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Or</cell>
                <cell><m>\vee</m></cell>
                <cell>
                    <m>P \vee Q</m> is true when at least one of <m>P</m> or
                    <m>Q</m> is true.
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Not (or Negation)</cell>
                <cell><m>\sim</m></cell>
                <cell>
                    <m> \neg P </m> has the opposite truth value of <m>P </m>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                    <line> Conditional Statement </line>
                    <line> <q>If...then</q> </line>
                </cell>
                <cell><m>\implies</m></cell>
                <cell>
                    <line> <m>P\implies Q</m> means <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q> and  is only false 
                      </line>
                    <line> in situations where <m>P</m> is true and <m>Q</m> is false. </line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Hypothesis</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    In a conditional statement
                    <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q>, <m>P</m> is the
                    hypothesis.
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Conclusion</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    In a conditional statement
                    <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q>, <m>Q</m> is the
                    conclusion.
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>
                    <line> Biconditional Statement </line>
                    <line> <q> if and only if</q>, or <q> iff </q> </line>
                </cell>
                <cell><m>\Leftrightarrow</m></cell>
                <cell>
                    <line> A biconditional statement is of the form <m>P</m> if and
                    only if <m>Q</m>. </line>
                    <line> It means <m>P\implies Q</m> and <m>Q\implies P</m>. </line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Converse</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    The converse of <m>P\implies Q</m> is <m>Q\implies P</m>.
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Logically Equivalent</cell>
                <cell><m>\Leftrightarrow</m></cell>
                <cell>
                    <line> A statement is <em>logically equivalent</em> to another
                    statement if they</line>
                    <line>  have the same truth value on all possible
                    inputs. </line>
                    <line> E.g.,
                    <m>\sim (P \wedge Q) \Leftrightarrow \sim P \,\vee \sim Q</m>
                    by DeMorgan's Laws </line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>For all</cell>
                <cell><m>\forall</m></cell>
                <cell>Also, ``For ever", ``For each"</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>There exists</cell>
                <cell><m>\exists</m></cell>
                <cell>Also ``For some"</cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Quantifier</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    <line> Indicates the number or quantity of our objects. </line>
                    <line>  <m>\forall</m> is the universal quantifier and</line>
                    <line> <m>\exists</m> is the existential quantifier </line>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Tautology</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    A statement that is true no matter what, e.g.,
                    <m>S\vee \sim S</m>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Contradiction</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    A statement that is never true, e.g., <m>S\wedge \sim S</m>
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row bottom="minor">
                <cell>Converse</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    The converse of <m>P\implies Q</m> is <m>Q\implies P</m>.
                </cell>
            </row>

            <row>
                <cell>Contrapositive</cell>
                <cell/>
                <cell>
                    The contrapositive of <m>P\implies Q</m> is
                    <m>\sim Q \implies \sim P</m>.
                </cell>
            </row>
        </tabular>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Truth Tables</title>

        <p>
            We use truth tables to organize information about the truth value of
            complicated statements based on the truth values of the pieces of
            that statement.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Fill in the following truth table.
                </p>

                <table>
                    <title>Tables for <q>And</q>, <q>Or</q></title>

                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>Q</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>P\wedge Q</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>P\vee Q</m></cell>
                        </row>

                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell>True</cell>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                        </row>

                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell>True</cell>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                        </row>

                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell>False</cell>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                        </row>

                        <row>
                            <cell>False</cell>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                            <cell/>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Fill in the following truth table.
                </p>

                <table>
                    <title>Table for <q>Not</q></title>

                    <tabular halign="center">
                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                            <cell><m>\neg P</m></cell>
                        </row>

                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell>True</cell>
                            <cell/>
                        </row>

                        <row bottom="minor">
                            <cell>False</cell>
                            <cell/>
                        </row>
                    </tabular>
                </table>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose <m>P</m> is the statement <q> <m>15</m> is odd</q>
                    and <m>Q</m> is the statement <q> <m>15</m> is prime</q>.
                    What are some true statements we can form using
                    <m>P, Q, \wedge, \vee, \sim</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Conditional Statements, Biconditional Statements</title>

        <p>
            We use conditional and biconditional statements in every day life!
            E.g.,
            <q>You can only get the math department scholarship if you apply </q>,
            or
            <q> You get SWS credit for MTH 210 pass if and only if you earn a C or better</q>.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let's return to an activity we did on the first day of class.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    A mathematician places a set of four cards on a table, each
                    of which has a number on one side and a colored patch on the
                    other side.
                    She claims the following:
                </p>

                <p>
                    if a card shows an even number on one face, then its
                    opposite face is red
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The visible faces of the cards show 3, 8, red, and blue.
                        Which card(s) must you turn over in order to test the
                        truth of her claim?
                        Carefully explain out loud to the people at your table.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>P</m> be the statement
                        <q>a card shows an even
                            number</q>
                        and <m>Q</m> be the statement
                        <q>it's
                            opposite face is red</q>.
                        Next to the <m>P</m> 's and <m>Q</m> 's above, label T,
                        F, or ?
                        for what you know about that card.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>

            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        What is/are the only situation(s) where the
                        mathematician could be lying, i.e., <m>P\implies Q</m>
                        would be false?
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    It is a well known law in the United States that you must be
                    21 to consume alcohol.
                    Suppose now that four cards are placed on the table each of
                    which has an age on one side and a beverage on the other.
                    The visible faces of the cards show 16, 22, coca-cola, and
                    beer.
                    If each card represents a person at the party, which card(s)
                    must you check to make sure everyone at the party is a law
                    abiding citizen.
                    That is, which card(s) must you turn over to verify the
                    proposition:
                </p>

                <p>
                    if one is consuming an alcoholic beverage then one is over
                    the age of 21
                </p>

                <p>
                    Complete the same tasks as in part 1, except now <m>P</m> is
                    <q>one is consuming an alcoholic beverage</q> and <m>Q</m>
                    is <q>one is over 21</q>.
                    What is/are the situation(s) could be breaking the law?
                    How does this relate to the previous problem?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Conditional and Biconditional Statement Truth Tables</title>

        <p>
            Conditional statement truth tables can be a little tricky because we
            have to think abou the truth value of
            <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m> </q> when <m>P</m> is false.
            Like we could say
            <q>if the moon is made of cheese then DocK is a millionaire.</q>
            This statement is considered true no matter what, because the
            hypothesis is false.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Based on the logic from the previous page, fill in the following
                truth tables.
            </p>

            <p>
                <term>Conditional, implies, or <q> If <m>P</m> then <m>Q</m></q>:</term>
            </p>

            <tabular>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                <col halign="center"/>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>Q</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>P\implies Q</m></cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell/>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell/>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell/>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell/>
                </row>
            </tabular>

            <p>
                <term>Biconditional, if and only if, or <m>P\Leftrightarrow Q</m>:</term>
            </p>

            <tabular>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                <col halign="center"/>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>Q</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>P\implies Q</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>Q\implies P</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>(P\implies Q)\wedge (Q\implies P)</m></cell>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                </row>

                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell>True</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                </row>

                <row>
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell>False</cell>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                    <cell/>
                </row>
            </tabular>

            <p>
                You will encounter many ``if and only if" statements.
                E.g., in linear algebra:
                <em>``A square matrix is invertible if and only if it has a pivot in every column"</em>,
                or in geometry:
                <em>``a triangle has a pair of congruent sides if and only if it has a pair of congruent angles"</em>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>The Converse and the Contrapositive</title>

        <p>
            The converse and the contrapositve are similar sounding words, but
            differ in very important ways.
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>converse</term> of <m>P\implies Q</m> is
                        <m>Q\implies P</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>contrapositive</term> of <m>P\implies Q</m> is
                        <m>Q\implies P</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a truth table that includes columns for
                    <m>P\implies Q</m>, <m>Q\implies P</m>, and
                    <m>\sim Q \implies \sim P</m>.
                </p>

                <tabular>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center"/>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>Q</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>P\implies Q</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>Q\implies P</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sim Q</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sim P</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\sim Q \implies \sim P</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What do you notice about the converse and the contrapositive
                    from your truth table above?
                    Do they have the same or different truth values?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What can you say about the relationship between the original
                    statement and the converse?
                    The original statement and the contrapositive?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Negating Conditional Statements</title>

        <p>
            The <term>negation</term> of the statement <m>P</m> is the statement
            <q> not <m>P</m> </q> and is denoted by <m>\sim P</m>.
            The negation of <m>P</m>, <m>\sim P</m>, is true only when <m>P</m>
            is false, and <m>\sim P</m> is false only when <m>P</m> is true.
        </p>

        <p>
            We want to be able to write the negations of statements written in
            English, so that we can get the statement of opposite truth value.
            This will be helpful when doing proofs by contradiction.
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity>
        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    What have you done in the past to show that a conditional
                    statement is false?
                    What does this suggest about the negation of a conditional
                    statement?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Make a truth table with <m>P\implies Q</m>,
                    <m>\sim (P\implies Q)</m>, <m>\sim P \vee Q</m>, and
                    <m>P\wedge \sim Q</m>.
                    What do you notice?
                    What does this suggest about the negation of a conditional
                    statement?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    As the previous exercises suggest, <m>\sim(P\implies Q)</m>
                    is the statement <m>P\vee \sim Q</m>.
                    Negate the statements from the previous activity.
                    Are the negations true or false?
                    How does that relate to the truth value of the original
                    statement?
                </p>
            </introduction>

            <task>
                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Original Statement:</term> If <m>a = 5</m> then
                    <m>a^2 = 25</m>.
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    This statement is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Negation:</term> <fillin characters="100"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    The negation is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Original Statement:</term> If <m>a\neq b</m> then
                    <m>a^4 \neq b^4</m>.
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    This statement is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Negation:</term> <fillin characters="100"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    The negation is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>
            </task>

            <task>
                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Original Statement:</term> If <m>a</m> is an odd
                    integer then <m>3a</m> is an odd integer.
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    This statement is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    <term>Negation:</term> <fillin characters="100"/>
                </p>

                <p workspace=".25in">
                    The negation is <fillin characters="25"/>
                </p>
            </task>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>
            More Practice with Truth Tables and Logically Equivalent Statements
        </title>

        <p>
            This is one of the learning targets!
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the truth table to show that <m>(P\vee Q)\implies R</m>
                    is logically equivalent to
                    <m>(P\implies R) \wedge (Q\implies R)</m>.
                </p>

                <tabular>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center" right="minor"/>
                    <col halign="center"/>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>P</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>Q</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>R</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>P\vee Q</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>P\vee \implies R</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>P\implies R</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>Q\implies R</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>(P\implies R) \wedge (Q\implies R)</m></cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>True</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell>False</cell>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                        <cell/>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Do we have all possible truth values of <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>,
                    and <m>R</m> in the truth table above?
                    What do you notice about how they are arranged?
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Draw a truth table to show <m>P\implies (Q\vee R)</m> is
                    logically equivalent to <m>(P \wedge \sim Q) \implies R</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Make sure you break down each statement so you aren't doing
                    too many things at once!
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>
            More Practice with Conditional Statements and Logical Equivalences
        </title>

        <p>
            One hard part about reading and writing mathematics is that there
            are lots of ways to say the same thing!
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity workspace=".5in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>a</m> be a real number and let <m>f</m> be a real-valued
                function defined on an interval containing <m>x=a</m>.
                Consider the following conditional statement:
            </p>

            <p>
                If <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>x=a</m>, then <m>f</m> is
                continuous at <m>x=a</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Let <m>P</m> be <q> <m>f</m> is differentiable </q>, and
                <m>Q</m> be <q> <m>f</m> is continuous</q>.
                So the above statement is <m>P\implies Q</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                For each of the following write in symbols, using <m>P</m>,
                <m>Q</m>, and logical operators.
                Then, determine which statements are equivalent to
                <m>P\implies Q</m> and which are the negation?
                (Some may be neither.)
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is continuous at <m>x=a</m>, then <m>f</m> is
                    differentiable at <m>x=a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is not differentiable at <m>x=a</m>, then
                    <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=a</m>, then <m>f</m>
                    is not differentiable at <m>x=a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f</m> is not differentiable at <m>x=a</m> or <m>f</m> is
                    continuous at <m>x=a</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f</m> is not continuous at <m>x=a</m> or <m>f</m> is
                    differentiable at <m>x=a</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f</m> is differentiable at <m>x=a</m> and <m>f</m> is not
                    continuous at <m>x=a</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Applying Conditional Statements</title>

        <p>
            Here, we think about taking a conditional statement that we know is
            true, and applying it to some other situation.
            Sometimes this looks like using a lemma in a proof.
            Following is a statement of a theorem which can be proven using
            calculus or precalculus mathematics.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-quadvertex">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f</m> is a quadratic function of the form
                    <m>f(x) = ax^2 +bx + c</m> where <m>a,b,c\in\mathbb{R}</m>
                    and <m>a&lt;0</m>, then the function <m>f</m> has a maximum
                    value when <m>x= \frac{-b}{2a}</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity workspace=".5in">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Using <em>only</em> <xref ref="thm-quadvertex"/>, what can be
                concluded about the functions given by the following formulas?
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <p>
                <m>g(x) = -8x^2 +5x-2</m>
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                <m>h(x)=-\frac{1}{3}x^2 + 3x</m>
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                <m>k(x) = 8x^2 -5x-7</m>
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) = -4x+2</m>
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                <m>j(x) = -\frac{71}{99}x^2 + 210</m>
            </p>
        </task>

        <task>
            <p>
                <m>f(x) = -x^4 + x^3 + 9</m>
            </p>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>